A||||23||lowfunds||Import aftermarket brakes?||||1||
Z||000000||lowfunds||06-09-2001||04:16 PM||||Just curious if anyone has looked into aftermarket brake upgrades using the import parts. I'm not saying this to start a flame about imports, just wondering if anyone has looked into it. Because there is such a large number of companies making these parts. If not import parts I believe there is companies making them for cavilers also, there is a link in another thread. Just looking for possibilities. Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||TRiAD||06-09-2001||04:56 PM||||What kind of kit are you looking for?<P>What size rotor?<BR>X-drilled or slotted or both ot neither?<BR>1 or 2-piece rotor?<BR>4 or 6-piston caliper?<BR>With or witheout pads, S/S lines, DOT4 or 5 fluid?<P>What pricerange?<P>Anything can be made, with enough interest for one specific type of kit, I could spec out a kit. <BR>*Note, the pre-'88s would be a different kit than the '88s.*<P>email me with your answers to these questions, and I will post the response tallies. If we get enough interest, we could spec out a new Fiero-specific kit. <P>Thanks!<P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> <BR><I>Photoshop rendering of planned exterior modifications.</I>||||reg||4
Z||000002||Slammed Fiero||06-09-2001||07:59 PM||||One of the best swaps on the cheap is a 11.25" Vented conversion from a 89-94 lebaron. You will need caliper adaptors and front hubs. My personal setup uses 4 piston willwood calipers (dynalites). Braided lines , Wilwood polymatrix D pads forthe track. This is the biggest you can go without going full custom adaptors and rotors.<P>This will run around $1000 US to do..less if you go with Camaro Front Calipers. Stopping is on par with any c4 vette 84 to 96 and any c2 , non big red braked 911.<P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Neal||06-09-2001||08:36 PM||||Hmmm, lowfunds i was thinking that myself.  But most imports are a four bolt pattern...<P>If i ever get some time of severe bordom i might look into somthing like that||||reg||1
Z||000004||TRiAD||06-09-2001||09:19 PM||||For $1K, I could build a complete 12" 4-piston kit with S/S lines, DOT5 fluid, pads, and cad-plated rotors (x-drilled, slotted, both or just vented), and all necessary hardware. <P>Just a thought. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||06-09-2001||11:27 PM||||Hey Slammed,did the LeBaron rotors fit onto the Fiero hub once the original rotor was turned/cut off?<BR>Also am wondering what kind of master cylinder you use.<BR>I have some Subaru 10.5" rotors,and BMW 4 piston calipers I've been thinking about trying to swap,but the larger rotors would help my caliper mounting situation.||||reg||1
Z||000006||lowfunds||06-10-2001||11:51 AM||||Triad that's about the figure I was coming up with. Although the two piece rotors look sweet I am thinking about them also. The big brake conversions with two piece rotors go for about $1200 I beleive. I think that some of the imports have the same lug pattern also. I will check and see. Any Ideas you come up with let me know. I am also looking into aftermarket coilovers, that are already 1 1/2" shorter when mounted so there is no need to turn them down. This will allow a better ride. However you can turn them down slightly if a firmer ride is desired.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bcwawright||06-10-2001||12:19 PM||||I am also interested in a big brake conversion. I want 13" two piece rotors(slotted and cross drilled)with 4 or 6 piston billet calipers. I have a 88GT and would like to know if there is a conversion kit or if someone just makes the adapter plates/brackets and I can buy my own disc and calipers. Please let me know.<BR>Thanks<P>Bruce<BR>88GT||||reg||6
Z||000008||Slammed Fiero||06-10-2001||12:25 PM||||I would like to know where some of you guys get your prices.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.movit.de" TARGET=_blank>www.movit.de</A> <P>for the big spenders. 911 big reds for a fiero. $3000 or so US dollars, for just the fronts.  No one makes better brakes than porsche.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">     <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals..Autocrossed , Tracked , abused.||||reg||1
Z||000009||sjp777||06-10-2001||12:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bcwawright:<BR><B>I am also interested in a big brake conversion. I want 13" two piece rotors(slotted and cross drilled)with 4 or 6 piston billet calipers. I have a 88GT and would like to know if there is a conversion kit or if someone just makes the adapter plates/brackets and I can buy my own disc and calipers. Please let me know.<BR>Thanks<P>Bruce<BR>88GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/brake13.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/brake13.htm</A> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowfunds||06-10-2001||01:45 PM||||As for price, they are in any sport/compact car mag for 550 for the front. Includes ss lines, rotors, pads, calipers and mounting hardware. Not sure exactly what size, I believe 12". I was just wondering how easy they could be adapted over. The whole point is using a kit that is popular already for the import crowd, so price will not be $3000. If anyone works at a tire/rim shop and can find the imports that have the same 5-lug spacing as the fiero that would be great.||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||06-10-2001||02:38 PM||||Yeah, 2-piece kits would be closer to $1200. <BR>The kits out there for imports are usually <B>for show only</B>, and do NOT work much better than kits that you could do for a lot less money. <P>If we spec'ed out a 12 or 13" kit, it would be specifically for function, not form. <BR>The set we did for our del Sol project would pick the back wheels OFF THE GROUND under hard braking, and any kind of a decline. These WORK.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>FYI. ||||reg||1
